            {"heading": "What Good Hygiene Actually Looks Like", "body": "Good password hygiene means long, unique passwords for each account, ideally generated or stored with a password manager rather than memorized and reused. It also means changing a password immediately after any suspected leak, and never sharing a password with someone else, even someone trusted, since that removes any ability to track who actually accessed an account."},
            {"heading": "This Connects to the Cert Track", "body": "Password hygiene is a core topic in the CareerSafe Cybersecurity/Networking certification this phase builds toward. It is also one of the simplest, cheapest security improvements a school can make, since it costs nothing and does not require new hardware, just better habits."},
